{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfxpvrqvWXFF6kj6tEdk1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All four steps are done, and reading the dashboard and the rule back shows the expected results.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Logs' (index `kibana_sample_data_logs`) uses `timestamp` as its time field. It also has an `@timestamp` field, but that is not the time field. The response code is stored as text, so I used its `response.keyword` field.\n2. **Dashboard:** I created 'S4-6675e2e1 Web logs' ([link](http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/s4-6675e2e1-web-logs-49c88bbb42b1)) with exactly two panels:\n   - \"Requests over time\": a line chart of request counts over `timestamp`.\n   - \"Requests by HTTP response code\": a bar chart of request counts for each `response.keyword` value.\n3. **Alert rule:** I created 'S4-6675e2e1 5xx errors' (id `be75dcaa-de9e-4b2f-89c4-9f555bab03d0`), and it was enabled from the start. It checks `kibana_sample_data_logs` every minute and fires when more than 10 matching requests arrive within 5 minutes.\n   - Because the response code is text, a plain number range wouldn't work. The rule instead matches `response.keyword` against the pattern `5[0-9][0-9]`, which covers every code from 500 to 599.\n4. **Edit and check:** I renamed the response-code panel to \"Responses by code\", and the dashboard now shows that title. Reading the rule back, it is enabled with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":255,"cache_read_input_tokens":21290,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":255},"output_tokens":546,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0da1e72d-e9d9-5